# SHAPE-GATE.allow — the complete register of hand-coded shape knowledge.
#
# The estate has ONE benchmark: this repo's data-driven bench. Shape knowledge
# belongs in bench/corpus/*.schema and in the code the compiler generates from
# it — nowhere else. bench/tools/shapegate enforces that mechanically, and
# every place that does not yet comply is listed HERE, by name, with a count
# and a reason. Nothing is exempt silently.
#
# FORMAT, one per line:
#
#     <check> <path> <count> <reason>
#
#     check   names | timing | paths | consts
#     count   the EXACT number of hits the gate finds today
#
# THE COUNT IS A RATCHET. More hits than the count is a refusal: the debt grew.
# FEWER hits is also a refusal, and the gate prints the new number to write:
# the debt shrank and the ledger has to say so. An entry matching no file is a
# refusal too. So the register cannot rot, and it only moves one way.
#
# Regenerate the lines after paying debt down:
#
#     go run ./bench/tools/shapegate -ledger
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DEBT IS PAID. Read this before reading the lines.
#
# When this gate was first written on main@6e1002b the register carried 2080
# name hits across twelve runner files, because every one of the nine legs
# hand-wrote the four corpus shapes: a family `rt` transcription against the
# runtime API, and a family `gen` pinned initializer plus LCG vary mapping per
# shape per language. That was the drift the owner named on 2026-08-31.
#
# #199 deleted family rt and the §1.3 hand-coded shapes. #204 retired the §1.2
# example-corpus rows from every runner. #201 deleted the decomp.cpp scratch
# harness. What is left is 29 name hits, and NOT ONE OF THEM IS HAND-CODED
# SHAPE MEASUREMENT. Every remaining line below is one of exactly three kinds,
# and each line says which:
#
#   (comment) the shape's NAME written in a prose comment. The gate is lexical
#             and does not strip comments — deliberately, since a comment is
#             where a hand-coded bench announces itself.
#   (callsite) the GENERATED type or symbol named at the point the shape-blind
#             driver instantiates it and hands it to generated Write/Read. The
#             driver has to name the generated symbol to call it; it writes no
#             field and pins no value. This is the design, not the debt.
#   (prose)   an ENGLISH word that is also a corpus field name — `extra`,
#             `sequence` — caught in a comment about something else. A known
#             false-positive class, named in the package doc's stoplist note.
#
# The nine legs are shape-blind. The gate now guards a clean tree, and the
# ratchet holds it there: any leg that grows one hand-written field, pin or
# wire size fails this gate on the next push.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

names bench/c/bench_main.c 4 shape-blind leg: 3 comments naming the shape + `#define BM_TYPE BenchMixed`, the generated type handed to the data-driven driver (callsite)
names bench/cpp/bench_main.cpp 3 shape-blind leg: 2 comments + `bench::BenchMixed` as the template argument to bench_datadriven (callsite)
names bench/cs/src/Program.cs 3 shape-blind leg: 1 comment + `Bench.BenchMixed` as the BenchDataDriven type argument (callsite), + `extra` in "one extra untimed pass" (prose)
names bench/dart/main.dart 3 shape-blind leg: 1 comment + `BenchMixed` as the gateDataDriven type argument and `BenchMixed.new` constructor tear-off (callsite)
names bench/elixir/main.exs 1 shape-blind leg: the header comment naming the shape this leg measures (comment)
names bench/elixir/runner.exs 2 shape-blind leg: 1 header comment (comment) + `sequence` in "replaying one sequence the branch predictor could memorize" (prose)
names bench/go/main.go 3 shape-blind leg: 1 comment + `bench.BenchMixed` as the benchDataDriven type argument (callsite), + `extra` in "one extra untimed pass" (prose)
names bench/java/Main.java 6 shape-blind leg: 1 comment + 5 `Bench.BenchMixed` callsites — the instance array, the decode target, and the generated read/write calls. Java has no generics over the generated type, so the driver names it inline instead of once (callsite)
names bench/js/main.mjs 2 shape-blind leg: 1 comment + `bench.BenchMixed` passed to benchDataDrivenFlat (callsite)
names bench/rust/src/main.rs 2 shape-blind leg: 1 comment + `benchgen::BenchMixed` as the bench_datadriven type parameter (callsite)

consts bench/c/bench_main.c 1 a comment stating the variant file is 64 x 438 bytes; no 438 in code (comment)
consts bench/java/Main.java 3 three references to GitHub issue #156, which collides with the declared 156-bit total of a corpus shape. A pure false positive of the numeric check, kept visible rather than special-cased (prose)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CORPUS TOOLING — shape knowledge in ONE place, deliberately.
#
# variantgen holds the BenchMixed pin and vary mapping so the nine legs do not
# have to: it commits the variant corpus every leg then reads blind. That is
# the design, not the debt — one copy replacing nine. It is listed anyway,
# because "one deliberate copy" is a claim that should be checkable rather
# than remembered. #195's emitter supersedes it.
#
# realpacket-gen is the same bargain for the §1.7 RealWorld snapshot.
# inline-gate names shapes only as SYMBOL NAMES in its budget symbol map and
# its test fixtures — no serialization, no timing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

names bench/tools/variantgen/main.go 94 the single deliberate copy of BenchMixed shape code; emits the corpus the nine legs read blind (superseded by #195)
names bench/tools/realpacket-gen/main.go 11 the single deliberate copy of the RealWorld snapshot shape code (§1.7)
names bench/tools/realpacket-gen/pin_test.go 3 pins the generator above against its golden
names bench/tools/inline-gate.sh 9 shape names inside the inline-budget symbol map and its disassembly test fixtures — row labels, no serialization, no clock
consts bench/tools/variantgen/main.go 3 three comments stating the 438-byte golden size this tool is the authority for (comment)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE §1.5 GOLDEN PINNER — correctness, not measurement.
#
# test/bench/ writes the pinned instances through the GENERATED code and
# byte-checks them against testdata/wire/. It is the oracle every runner is
# gated against before it is allowed to report a number. It names shapes, as a
# conformance suite must, and it contains no clock — which is exactly the line
# this gate draws. Only its bench-shaped PATH trips a check.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

paths test/bench/c_main.c 1 the §1.5 golden pinner: correctness code, no timing
paths test/bench/main.cpp 1 the §1.5 golden pinner: correctness code, no timing

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A CALENDAR DATE IS NOT A CLOCK.
#
# The tables baseline (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §18.4) stamps each history entry with the
# day it was written, so a person reading the intentional-break log years
# later knows when the break happened. It measures no interval and times
# nothing; the timing check is lexical and cannot tell a date from a clock.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

timing internal/baseline/file.go 1 the history entry's calendar date — a day written into a text log, no interval measured (prose)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GATE 2 — the spec's own MEASURED gate (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §12.1).
#
# The one-benchmark rule is about the SERIALIZE benchmark: nine hand-written
# approximations of one measurement, drifting apart. These two files measure
# something else entirely and measure it once, in one place, on both sides of
# one boundary — the block form's per-frame C++ WRITE and per-frame C# READ,
# each against the hand-written form it replaces, which is the bar §12.1 sets
# and the evidence that decides whether tables earn the render data at all.
#
# They are not `make test`; they are `make tables-block-gate2`, run under the
# bench rules (gated goldens first — each half refuses to bench on a mismatch
# — arms interleaved in one sitting, medians paired). The shapes they name are
# the block corpus's, not bench/corpus's, so the NAMES check does not fire;
# only the clock does, and the clock is the point.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

timing test/tables/block_gate2_main.cpp 6 gate 2's C++ half (§12.1): the per-frame write, generated against the hand-written scatter
timing test/cs-block/src/Gate2.cs 2 gate 2's C# half (§12.1): the per-frame read, generated against the hand mirror

# THE COOK's OPEN-COST GATE (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §7, §7.5) measures the ABSENCE of
# work, which is why it needs a clock and why it is not a bench. `Open` is
# O(1) in the file's size — a header match and nothing per node — and the gate
# is that a 1 MB cook and a 100 MB cook open in the SAME time. It names no
# shape and reads no field: the two arms are the same generated `SceneOpen`
# over two synthetic regions, paired in one sitting and reported as medians,
# and what a red arm would mean is that somebody put a walk back.

timing test/tables/cook_main.cpp 2 the cook's open-cost gate (§7, §7.5): open time flat across 1 MB and 100 MB, paired medians — a constant-time header match, no shape named
timing test/cs-cook/src/Program.cs 3 the cook's open-cost gate on the C# leg (§7, §7.5): the same two fixtures, the same paired medians, one warm-up pass because a tiered runtime measured cold measures tier-up — a constant-time header match, no shape named
timing test/conformance/elixir/driver_impl.ex 3 the Elixir SOAK's deadline and its interval reports (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md, the reading tier's allocation budget): a clock that decides when to stop and when to print, never a rate — the soak's instrument is the live heap and the binary memory against a warm baseline, and the COUNT half beside it (make tables-elixir-alloc-audit) uses no clock at all
timing test/conformance/rust/src/main.rs 3 the Rust SOAK's deadline and its interval reports (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md, "every read path allocates nothing"): a clock that decides when to stop and when to print, never a rate — the soak's instrument is LIVE ALLOCATED BYTES and it emits no timing number at all

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TABLES LEG (bench/tables/README.md).
#
# The same design as the nine type legs and the same three kinds of entry: a
# shape-blind runner has to NAME the generated type at the point it hands it
# to the generated Save/Load, and it names nothing else about the shape — no
# field, no pinned value, no wire size. The corpus's own producer is the one
# place that knows the shape, and it carries no clock.
#
# The gate guards this corpus at all because `table` now rides in the
# declaration keywords it extracts from bench/corpus/*.schema; before that a
# table's name was invisible to it, and a leg could have hand-written one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

names bench/tables/cpp/table_main.cpp 4 shape-blind leg: 1 comment + `benchtable::TableMixed` as the bench_table template argument and its Reset/Save/Load call sites (callsite)
names bench/tables/cs/src/Program.cs 2 shape-blind leg: `Benchtable.TableMixed` as the BenchTable type argument and its constructor (callsite)
names bench/tables/dart/table_main.dart 2 shape-blind leg: `TableMixed` as the benchTable type argument and its constructor tear-off — its reset/save/load are METHODS on the value, taken as closures the corpus vocabulary does not spell (callsite)
names bench/tables/go/table_main.go 2 shape-blind leg: `benchtable.TableMixed` as the benchTable type argument and in the one load closure's signature (callsite)
names bench/tables/js/main.mjs 1 shape-blind leg: `TableMixed` once, as the constructor the four generated call sites are named from (callsite)
names bench/tables/rust/src/main.rs 1 shape-blind leg: `TableMixed` as the bench_table type argument, once — its reset/save/load are snake_case free functions the corpus vocabulary does not spell, so the Rust leg names the shape in exactly one place (callsite)
names bench/tables/java/src/Main.java 2 shape-blind leg: `benchtable.BenchTableTable.TableMixed` as the benchTable type argument and its constructor reference (callsite)
consts bench/tables/java/src/Main.java 1 the round-robin variant mask `(i & (NUM_VARIANTS - 1))` folds to a literal the numeric check reads as a corpus bit total. A pure false positive, kept visible rather than special-cased (prose)
names bench/tables/c/table_main.c 3 shape-blind leg: C has no template to pass the type through, so `TableMixed` is named where the storage is declared and at its Reset/Save/Load call sites — no field, no pinned value, no wire size (callsite)

# The tables corpus's golden pinner and oracle: correctness code, no clock.
# It is the ONE place that names a field of BenchTable.schema, which is what
# lets both legs above read the committed data blind. Only its bench-shaped
# PATH trips a check, exactly as its type twin's does.

paths test/bench/table_main.cpp 1 the tables corpus's producer and §1.5 oracle: correctness code, no timing

# THE GO PORT's SOAK (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md, "What allocates, and what never
# does") reads a clock for the same reason the cook's open-cost gate does: to
# BOUND A DURATION, not to measure one. What it watches is
# runtime.MemStats.Mallocs across the loop, which must not move at all; the two
# clock reads decide when to stop. It names no shape it did not get from the
# conformance corpus's own manifest, and it reports no time.

timing test/dart-tables/soak.dart 1 the Dart port's soak: one Stopwatch BOUNDS the run, and what is watched is a BYTE COMPARISON of every round trip; no time is reported and no shape is measured
timing test/go-tables/soak_test.go 2 the Go port's allocation soak: the clock BOUNDS the loop, the allocation counter is what is watched — no time is reported and no shape is measured
timing test/java-tables/src/Main.java 7 the Java tables leg's SOAK and ALLOCATION gates: a wall clock bounding a duration and a warm-up window, over a corpus it never names — no shape measured, no rate reported
